Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md, judging from the diff and the commit messages. I didn't run the binary or re-run the tests myself.

• **What the fix does:** in single-prompt mode (`-p` or a piped prompt), Ctrl+C now prints `(interrupted)` to stderr instead of the "press Ctrl+C again" hint to stdout, and the process exits 130. Text that already streamed stays on stdout.
• **REPL unchanged:** the REPL still gets the old hint through `write_chrome`, byte for byte. In `main.rs`, `mark_single_shot()` is called only in the `-p` and piped branches, so the mode depends on how yoyo was started, not on whether stdout is a terminal.
• **Piped auto-continue:** piped mode no longer auto-continues after an interrupt.
• **Tests:**
  • An end-to-end test waits for the streamed text on stdout, then sends SIGINT. It asserts stdout is exactly that text, stderr contains (interrupted), and the exit code is 130.
  • A second test confirms an uninterrupted -p run still prints the same text and exits 0.
  • A table test covers the REPL and single-prompt notes.
• **Commit message:** it records two positive controls (deliberately break the fix, confirm the test fails, restore). They reproduced the original #993 output byte for byte and the old exit code 0.
• **Docs:** there's a #993 line under [Unreleased] in CHANGELOG.md, and docs/src/usage/single-prompt.md now documents exit 130 on interrupt.

Two minor gaps, neither enough to fail it:
• The commit message I read doesn't say what exit code the JSON modes give after an interrupt, which the task asked for.
• --print has no SIGINT end-to-end test, because it doesn't stream anything to wait on. The commit says this plainly.
